{{en-adj|trigger}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|_|dialectal}} [[true|True]]; [[trusty]]; [[trustworthy]]; [[faithful]].
# {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|_|dialectal}} [[safe|Safe]]; [[secure]].
# {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|_|dialectal}} [[tight|Tight]]; [[firm]]; [[steady]]; [[sound]]; in good condition or health.
# [[neat|Neat]]; [[tidy]]; [[trim]]; [[spruce]]; [[smart]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1857|author=J. Rarey|chapter=The Taming of Horses|title=British Quarterly Review
|passage=we possess of pig's skin and stirrups to keep them square and '''trig'''}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|IX|passage=“A tight little craft,” was Austin’s invariable comment on the matron; and she looked it, always trim and '''trig''' and smooth of surface like a converted yacht cleared for action. &para; Near her wandered her husband, orientally bland, invariably affable,{{nb...}}.}}
#* '''1973''', ''{{w|Newsweek}}'', April 16 <!-- Cited as a late example of usage in a major magazine -->
#*: The [torture] stories seemed incongruent with the men telling them – a trim, '''trig''' lot who, given a few pounds more flesh, might have stepped right out of a recruiting poster.
